The approval execution doesn't actually apply the state change (the audit noted this: "the resolution never executes"). Let me add the execution: when a GATEWAY_VERSION approval is approved, promote the version to LIVE, set the gateway's activeVersionId, supersede the previous version, and publish the invalidation.

That's an important correctness fix (maker-checker must actually execute the change).

Let me implement it:
```ts
if (approval.subjectType === "GATEWAY_VERSION") {
  const version = await client.gatewayVersion.findUnique({where: {id: approval.subjectId}});
  if (version) {
    await client.$transaction(async (tx) => {
      // supersede the previously live version
      await tx.gatewayVersion.updateMany({
        where: { gatewayId: version.gatewayId, state: "LIVE", id: { not: version.id } },
        data: { state: "SUPERSEDED" },
      });
      await tx.gatewayVersion.update({ where: { id: version.id }, data: { state: "LIVE" } });
      await tx.gateway.update({ where: { id: version.gatewayId }, data: { activeVersionId: version.id, status: "LIVE" } });
    });
    await invalidationBus.invalidateGateway(version.gatewayId, "gateway-version-live");
  }
}
```

Careful: `gateway.activeVersionId` is `@unique`. When we set the new active version, the old gateway row's activeVersionId is replaced — fine since it's on the gateway, not the version. But `gateway_versions.activeForGateway` is a 1-1 relation via `Gateway.activeVersionId @unique`. Setting it replaces the pointer. OK.

Also `version.state` is now `GatewayVersionState` which has LIVE ✓ and SUPERSEDED ✓.

Also `gateway.status` is `GatewayStatus` which has LIVE ✓.

Let me also handle the rollback route (`gateways/:id/rollback`) to set the target version LIVE and mark the rolled-back one ROLLED_BACK.

Let me look at the rollback handler.

